### workspace/gf17_auto_v3.py - GF17 Recurrent Texel Automaton (generation)
Generation-first GF17 substrate. Recurrent GF17 state (K channels x 17 values) advanced by texel-map folds; palette-fetch readout.
Env knobs: K, L, B, S1, S2, LR, TAU0, TAU1, LAY, WPL, SEED. Writes gf17_auto_v3.json + gf17_auto_v3_{soft,hard}.pt.
Companion feedforward path: workspace/gf17_fold_v3.py (CTX=8 greedy MI fold search, val 1.7132).

### workspace/gf17_core.py - shared GF17 generation core
Corpus, hard automaton load (gf17_auto_v3_hard.pt), batched `astep`, `allstates` blockwise state
pass, frame/digit builders, `DigitFold` readout, AdamW `joint` fitter (wd, ck=25, optional sample
weights), `replay` (batched fold-stack replay), `poolrow` (batched pool build). Env: CTX, WD, THREADS.
All later GF17 generation scripts import this — do not re-implement the automaton.

### workspace/gf17_emitgen_v1.py - 500-step emit harness (scoring instrument)
Scores the live generation loop, not val CE. Reports emit_tf / statedrift / fullfree / self_ce
separately, plus orbit closure (per-channel tail periodicity, frozen vs closed vs open, first
full-state repeat step). `emit_tf - off500 == 0.0000` is the pipeline fidelity assertion.
Checkpoints wire + Tmaps + numbers to gf17_emitgen_v1.json after **every** fold (v1 of statefold
died at fold05 and lost its whole fold stack). Env: M, EMIT, CAND, SUBN, WIN, WD.

### workspace/gf17_selfstate_v2.py - self-state fitting (the exposure fix)
Batched self-driven rollouts (RB parallel trajectories x RL steps, state seeded from SALL, advanced
by sampled chars, context and target true) appended to the training pool at 3x weight, refit each
round. Trades teacher-forced CE for open-loop robustness: +0.194 tf buys -1.314 statedrift over 3
rounds. Also emits the drift curve CE(h) for h in 1..128. Env: ITS, RB, RL, EMIT, THREADS.

### workspace/gf17_selfgrow_v1.py - fold growth ON the self distribution (2026-08-17)
The fold stack is grown against DAgger-harvested drifted rows, not teacher-forced rows. Every fold
step: harvest RB parallel free-running trajectories x RL steps (state seeded exactly from SALL, no
rewarm), keep the last KEEP harvests at weight ALPHA, then run wire selection (weighted MI) AND the
exact-289 T solve AND the head refit on the mixed pool. Two arms in one process (`tf` control vs
`mix`) share seed, drift positions and emit starts. Scoreboard is generation-only: drift ladder
CE(h) over 96 lanes x 256 free steps, corpus-trigram judge of a 500-char free emit with a real-text
floor and a unigram ceiling, distinct/novel 8-gram ratios. Env: M, RB, RL, KEEP, ALPHA, DB, DL, EB,
EL, ST0, SUBN. Weighted fitpal1/exactT/foldfit/wscore now live in gf17_core.py (single copy).

### workspace/gf17_chainfold_v1.py - ancestral GF(17) digit chain readout (2026-08-17)
Kills the factored 95-way readout. `ChainFold` is two texel heads: `h1` reads the fold pool -> d1
(6-way); the chosen d1 is then APPENDED TO THE POOL AS A GF(17) TEXEL COLUMN and `h0` reads
pool+d1(+B-folds) -> d0 (17-way). `clogc` composes the exact 95-way joint ancestrally over the 6
d1 values, so p(c) = p(d1)p(d0|d1) instead of p(d0)p(d1). Growth runs on BOTH stages: A-folds
(289->17 Tmap, MI-scored against d1) feed h1; B-folds wire the d1 column against context columns
(`bwire`, MI against d0) and feed h0 only - that is conditioning d0 on d1 through a Tmap, not a
second head. Arms `fact` (exact prior factored product) and `chain` share seed, harvested rows,
wire search and eval. Judge is reported with sample entropy H1 against a real-text H_real so a
collapsed emitter cannot fake a win. Env: M, ARMS, TEMPS, SUF, RB, RL, KEEP, ALPHA, DB, DL, EB,
EL, ST0, CK, SUBN. Saves gf17_chainfold_{fact,chain}_base.pt.
gf17_core.py v1.1.0: fitpal1/exactT/foldfit take `ls=` (loss fn) and size Pal from base.shape[1];
wscore takes `nc=` (target cardinality); replay no longer assumes an NBASE dummy wire prefix.

### workspace/gf17_texbank_v1.py - GF(17) texel PAGE BANK readout (2026-08-17)
Replaces the additive digit readout with a bank of GF(17) texel pages. A page is a 289-entry map
addressed by a DIGIT PAIR `z[i]*17 + z[j]` and fetched by index; logits are the sum of P pages
plus the order-1 digit terms, so the whole readout is gather+segment-sum (EmbeddingBag), never a
matmul. Pairs are chosen by SYNERGY `MI(i,j) - MI(i) - MI(j)` on loss-weighted rows, not raw MI,
so only genuine conjunctions get a page. Chain factorization from gf17_chainfold_v1 is kept: `h1`
pages -> d1, the d1 value is appended as texel column 32 and `h0` pages (B-side pairs FORCED to
include every `(i,32)`) -> d0. Zero-init pages mean round 0 starts exactly at the additive model.
`ARMS=chainbase` reloads gf17_chainfold_chain_base.pt and scores it through the identical harness,
so cross-wave numbers share one window. Period instrument: first exact `(ctx,St)` repeat over the
free window, frozen channel count, mean per-channel cardinality. Env: PA, PB, R, RB, RL, KEEP,
ALPHA, ST0, CK, LR, BS, DB, DL, EB, EL, TEMPS, ARMS, SUF. Saves gf17_texbank_r<round><SUF>.pt.
Champion: PA=48/PB=56 -> `gf17_texbank_r0_s48.pt`.

FRONTIER (one 500-char window, 4 lanes, T=1.0; real-text floor 1.9279, real-text new8 0.3613,
real-text uniq8 0.7809):

| readout | params | char_va | tf32 | judge | new8 | uniq8 | free500 | loop1 |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| chain M=0 (additive) | 12824 | 2.4694 | 2.5206 | 4.0609 | 0.9843 | 1.0 | 6.7235 | none |
| bank 16/24 | 197784 | 2.0014 | 2.0179 | 2.7701 | 0.8862 | 0.9949 | 7.9652 | 230 |
| **bank 48/56** | 371184 | **1.7233** | **1.7649** | **1.8829** | 0.5906 | 0.9797 | 10.5079 | 255 |
| bank 96/112 | 729544 | 1.9266 | 1.9936 | 1.9317 | 0.5122 | 0.9473 | 14.7145 | 227 |
| bank 224/256 | 1658968 | 2.2595 | 2.3801 | 1.7076 | 0.3508 | 0.9341 | 19.7807 | 112 |

Three settled facts from this table. (1) char_va is U-shaped in page count with a minimum at
48/56; the 2.13-nat judge gap the additive stack could not close was READOUT CAPACITY, not folds,
not temperature, not orbit structure. (2) `free_ce500` (free-run CE against the true held-out
continuation) is ANTI-CORRELATED with generation quality - it rises monotonically 6.72 -> 19.78 as
the judge falls 4.06 -> 1.71. It measures trajectory divergence from one specific document, so a
diffuse gibberish emitter wins it. It must not be used as a generation score. (3) Orbit closure is
a symptom of readout sharpness, not of quality: the additive stack never loops in 500 steps at any
fold count, every bank loops, and the loop arrives EARLIER as pages are added.
Novelty gate: real held-out text scores new8=0.3613 / uniq8=0.7809, so a run only counts as
generation (not recall) above those. 48/56 clears both while beating the real-text judge floor.
224/256 reaches judge 1.7076 but its new8=0.3508 sits under the real-text reference - capacity
past ~50 pages buys judge with recall.
DAgger/mix-row training on the bank is a DEAD LEVER for quality: rounds 0-3 walk judge
1.9317 -> 2.9883 -> 3.7324 -> 4.1972 while free500 falls 14.71 -> 8.32 -> 7.03 -> 6.64. It trades
the only meter that matters for the one that is anti-correlated with it.
Hard-folding pages (`workspace/gf17_texfold_v1.py`, Tmap 289->17 + 17-row palette per page) is a DEAD
LEVER at both scales: 48/56 judge 1.8829 -> 2.9035 (char_va 1.7233 -> 2.1150, params 371184 -> 33904),
224/256 judge 1.7076 -> 2.8445. Both folded arms are dominated on (judge, new8) by the 16-page bank.
Learning the recurrent latent tables by exactT descent (`workspace/gf17_texgen_v1.py`, L=16 channels,
palette SGD alternating with gradient-free 289-cell sweeps + self-drift rows) also LOSES: it descends
monotonically (2.9002 -> 2.7455 char_va) but stays 1.0 nat behind the frozen `gf17_auto_v3_hard.pt`
automaton under the identical singles readout. Do not re-derive either.
The whole board is one curve: over 20 arms, Spearman(judge, free500) = -0.973, judge ~ ln(free500)
slope -2.350, R2 0.737 (`workspace/gf17_frontier_v1.py`).
COPY-COVERAGE GATE (`workspace/gf17_copycheck_v1.py`) — this corpus is formulaic, so REAL held-out text
already scores cov16 0.493 / cov24 0.309 / longest verbatim run 55 chars. That is the control any low
judge must be read against. Champion 48/56: 0.436 / 0.178 / 33 (below real = genuine generation).
224/256: 0.740 / 0.346 / 58 and 496/512: 0.738 / 0.518 (above real = recall, disqualified).
RETRACTED 2026-08-17 by `workspace/gf17_gate_v1.py`: those coverage numbers were read at T=1.0, where
each arm sits at a DIFFERENT operating point, so the comparison was invalid. See MATCHED-COVERAGE GATE.

### workspace/gf17_gate_v1.py - MATCHED-COVERAGE GATE, the only valid generation scoreboard (2026-08-17)
Sets `ARMS=none` so `gf17_texbank_v1` imports as a library (TexBank + clogc, no training), reloads any
`gf17_texbank_*.pt`, and scores every arm through ONE harness at its OWN coverage-matched temperature:
binary-search T until cov16 equals the real held-out cov16, then report the rest. Rationale: temperature
trades copy-coverage against novelty monotonically, so any fixed-T table ranks operating points, not
models. REAL reference at the shared 4x500 window: judge 1.9279, H1 3.4473, cov16 0.5845, cov24 0.4200,
maxcopy 73, new8 0.3613, uniq8 0.7809. `GAP` = sum of relative |deviation| on (judge, H1, cov16, cov24).

| arm | char_va | free500 | T_match | judge | cov16 | cov24 | maxcopy | new8 | GAP |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| REAL held-out | - | - | - | 1.9279 | 0.585 | 0.420 | 73 | 0.361 | 0.000 |
| **bank 224/256** | 2.2595 | 19.78 | 1.012 | 1.7713 | 0.596 | 0.333 | 58 | 0.373 | **0.315** |
| bank 96/112 round3 | 2.0581 | 6.64 | 0.359 | 2.3074 | 0.526 | 0.331 | 52 | 0.490 | 0.528 |
| bank 96/112 round0 | 1.9266 | 14.71 | 0.857 | 1.6820 | 0.609 | 0.256 | 48 | 0.372 | 0.581 |
| bank 48/56 | 1.7233 | 10.51 | 0.720 | 1.5731 | 0.546 | 0.241 | 46 | 0.419 | 0.700 |
| bank 16/24 | 2.0014 | 7.97 | 0.359 | 1.5629 | 0.467 | 0.258 | 40 | 0.499 | 0.779 |

Two prior verdicts die here. (1) 224/256 is NOT disqualified for recall: at matched coverage it copies
LESS than real held-out text does (cov16 0.596 vs 0.585, maxcopy 58 vs 73, new8 0.373 vs 0.361) and it
is the best generator on disk. Its T=1.0 cov16 of 0.740 was a temperature artifact. (2) 48/56 is not the
champion on generation - it is 4th of 5. It wins char_va, which is a teacher-forced meter.
DAgger round3 is also rehabilitated as an OPERATING POINT, not a loss: at T=1.0 it is the worst arm on
the board (judge 4.1972) and at matched coverage it is 2nd (GAP 0.528). Any arm can be moved along the
coverage axis by T; only the shape of the trade is a property of the model.

### workspace/gf17_texaddr_v1.py - latent-ADDRESSED texel pages, zero gradient descent (2026-08-17)
DEAD LEVER. Pages are FETCHED BY the latent instead of summed alongside it. Fold cascade: pick the digit
pair with max loss-weighted MI on the target, cluster its 289 conditionals into 17 buckets by KL-Lloyd
(`G.cluster17`, exact MI-optimal hard assignment `argmax_b sum_y C[c,y] log q_b[y]`), append the folded
digit as a new GF(17) column, reweight rows by residual NLL, repeat - later pairs freely reach into
earlier folds (folds 5-9 picked `(ctx6,f2) (ctx7,f1) (f1,f2) (f2,f3)`). Then greedy-select A=4 columns as
a base-17 page ADDRESS and read a mip pyramid of count pages, level l backing off to level l-1 with
weight kappa. No SGD anywhere; pages are counts, folds are exact tabular argmins.
Result: the addressed bank LOSES to the additive bank 2-4x on the matched gate (best GAP 1.936 vs 0.315)
and never reaches cov24 > 0 at T=1.0. Three sub-results worth keeping:
- FOLD ADDRESSES ARE WORSE THAN RAW DIGITS. cand=fold char_va 2.1450, cand=raw 1.7423, cand=all picks
  mostly raw. Third independent loss for folding (after texfold pages and texgen latent tables).
- THE GREEDY SELECTOR REFUSES THE LATENT. Given all 32 columns free choice it took 4 CONTEXT digits
  (ctx7.d0, ctx7.d1, ctx6.d0, ctx5.d0) and ZERO state channels. state-only address char_va 2.3487,
  forced 2ctx+2state 2.3733, raw-4 1.7423. At h=1 the automaton state is dominated by more context.
- MIP BACKOFF DOES NOT FIX DRIFT. Hypothesis was that an off-manifold address degrades gracefully to a
  lower mip level; free500 is 13.1-14.9 vs the additive bank's 10.5. Refuted.

### workspace/gf17_horizon_v1.py - WHY the latent looked useless: it is a long-range channel (2026-08-17)
Bias-matched MI probe. For each horizon h, greedily build a 3-digit context address, then measure the MI
gain of a 4th digit taken from context vs from the automaton state - both land in an identical 17^4 cell
space on identical row counts, so the estimator bias cancels and the comparison is valid (the absolute
MI values at large h are inflated and must not be quoted).

| h | best1 ctx | best1 state | ctx3 | state3 | gain4 ctx | gain4 state | winner |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | 0.5218 | 0.4274 | 2.2681 | 1.9025 | +0.6338 | +0.4787 | ctx |
| 8 | 0.0798 | 0.0630 | 1.1674 | 0.9658 | +0.9501 | +0.6531 | ctx |
| 16 | 0.0306 | 0.0282 | 0.8963 | 0.7023 | +0.9390 | +0.7276 | ctx |
| 32 | 0.0172 | 0.0176 | 0.7060 | 0.5492 | +0.8044 | **+0.8836** | STATE |
| 64 | 0.0097 | 0.0108 | 0.5803 | 0.4575 | +0.7485 | **+0.8043** | STATE |
| 128 | 0.0110 | 0.0114 | 0.6106 | 0.5079 | +0.8231 | **+0.8991** | STATE |

Crossover at h=32. Every objective on this project has been h=1, which is exactly the regime where the
GF(17) latent is redundant with the context window - that is why 8 waves of readouts on top of it behave
like n-gram tables and why free generation loops.

### workspace/gf17_plansteer_v1.py - two-scale texel address: local page x latent horizon field (2026-08-17)
Acts on the crossover. Two banks, both pure counts, both TMU fetches: LOCAL addressed by the 4 context
digits on the h=1 target, PLAN addressed by 4 state channels on the BAG OF THE NEXT `WIN` CHARS. Emit
logits = `log p_local + beta * (log p_plan - log p_unigram)`, i.e. a product of experts where the latent
supplies only the tilt away from the marginal. Env: ADDR_L, ADDR_P, WIN, KAP, KAPP, BETAS.
Real but small, with an interior optimum, and it costs teacher-forced CE:

| WIN | beta | char_va | free500 | judge_m | cov16_m | cov24_m | new8_m | GAP_m |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| - | 0 (control) | 1.7423 | 13.12 | 1.3493 | 0.299 | 0.059 | 0.557 | 1.668 |
| 32 | 0.6 | 1.7654 | 13.62 | 1.3618 | 0.287 | 0.104 | 0.572 | 1.561 |
| 128 | 1.6 | 1.8270 | 13.43 | 1.4078 | 0.262 | 0.128 | 0.553 | 1.529 |
| 128 | **2.4** | 1.9114 | 13.65 | 1.3735 | 0.301 | **0.178** | 0.572 | **1.380** |
| 128 | 3.5 | 2.0641 | 13.99 | 1.5543 | 0.308 | 0.115 | 0.612 | 1.474 |
| 128 | 5.0 | 2.3487 | 14.56 | 1.8713 | 0.176 | 0.012 | 0.719 | 1.818 |

Latent horizon steering triples cov24 (0.059 -> 0.178) and cuts GAP 17% inside its own arm, and the
optimum sits at the horizon the MI probe predicted (WIN=128, past the h=32 crossover). It does NOT reach
the additive bank (GAP 1.380 vs 0.315), so the mechanism is proven and the carrier is wrong: the tilt
belongs INSIDE the additive bank's page set as horizon-target pages, not as a separate address.
gf17_core.py v1.2.0: shared generation metrics `judge/novel/hs/cover/longest/gstats/realstats/gap` and
shared page-bank primitives `pcount/pstep/plogp/rollgen/matchtemp/miw/cluster17`. `rollgen(fn, ...)`
takes a row->logits closure so every arm rolls through one code path. Verified by re-running texaddr
raw/k=2 post-refactor: char_va 1.7423, judge 1.7773, GAP 1.9997 - identical to pre-refactor.

### workspace/gf17_composegen_v1.py - COMPOSE: baked induction counts x emit-time GF17 page (2026-08-17)
The two generation mechanisms that survived the wave are orthogonal: `gf17_indfold_r0.pt` bakes
induction (matched-length q=8/5/3 back-references, W=384) into a trained char head, and the episodic
GF17 texel page is written DURING emission. Neither had been run composed. This script loads the
indfold head frozen (no retrain) and reuses the ORD/laneW from `gf17_epifield_seedcheck.pt` so the
page address is the already-proven LE=3 coarse lane address, not a new one.
Boost lives in char space: every candidate char v is pushed through `astep` (`nextlat`, B x V x K),
and the page counts of the resulting lane digits are gathered and mixed by the fitted lane weights:
  lg += alpha * einsum(W_k, log1p(page[b,k,eh(base_LE(Sd))_k])[..., nextlat(v)_k])
After each emit, the page is index_add'd at the realized lane state, so the generator accumulates a
texel record of its own trajectory and re-quotes itself. Temperature is coverage-matched to REAL
cov16 on the mean of 2 seeds (single-seed matching drifts the mean off target).
alpha=1.5 LE=3 t=0.943 is the optimum: uniq8 0.7414 (REAL 0.7819), cov16 0.5189 (REAL 0.4975),
judge 1.8579, page occ 0.053, ephit 0.289. That halves u8dist against both parents.
alpha=2.0 (the epifield-alone optimum) OVERSHOOTS on the composed head - it over-quotes to uniq8
0.6613 with SD 0.1136. The sharper the base head, the lower the correct alpha.

### workspace/gf17_trifuse_v1.py - NEGATIVE: the lane field does not transfer its low drift (2026-08-17)
Third additive term beta * log_softmax(einsum(W, lanelp(Sd).gather(nextlat))) on top of the compose.
The LV=4 lane field is the better PREDICTOR (val CE 1.6992 vs indfold char_va 2.1665) and the worse
GENERATOR: beta=0.5 pushes drift 21.0 -> 24.5, beta=1.0 -> 29.8 with judge 1.5396, below the trigram
floor 1.9296, i.e. it collapses into corpus phrases, and the cov16 temperature match hits the 1.45
search ceiling. Do not re-fuse the lane field into a char head at emit time.

## Ray hash-field byte LM (2026-09-19, v6.21.11)

- `scripts/ray_hash_field_ti_v3.py` — the engine. Env: `CORPUS` (big|big2|code|small), `LEVELS` (8/12/16 tori), `LOG2_ENTRIES` (cells per torus), `BS`, `STEPS` (also sets the LR schedule length), `LR`, `LR_END`, `NO_MOMENTUM=1` (two fields instead of three, needed at 2^18), `PAGES=<_pages.npy>` with `STEPS=0` for offline generation, `GEN_K`, `GEN_T`. Writes `exports/gf17_continuum/rayhash_<TAG>.json/.log/_pages.npy/_bias.npy`.
- `scripts/ray_hash_field_ti_v4.py` — v3 + learned per-ray phase (two-corner torus reads, exact phase gradient, `PH_LR`). Not a gain at matched schedule.
- `scripts/ray_hash_field_ti_v5.py` — v3 with two S² sphere walks (per-byte and per-word rotations, cube-map bilinear reads, `SPHERE_RES/SPHERE_A/SPHERE_W`) in place of the topic torus. Not a gain at matched schedule.
- `scripts/ray_hash_field_ti_v6.py` — v4 + v5. Worse.
- `scripts/ray_hash_field_ti_v7.py` — slot pages: SLOTS candidates per cell (id by replacement RTH/RW, weight by Adam, WD decay); `TERN=1` trit-aware forward (`TERN_SCOPE=cell|level`, `TERN_TH`), `GATE` inference-only gain gate (`GATE_TRAIN=1` to gate in training, loses). Saves `_sid.npy/_sw.npy/_bias.npy`; `PAGES=<_sid.npy> STEPS=0` evaluates.
- `scripts/ray_hash_field_ti_v9.py` — hybrid: exact dense byte1 (256) and byte2 (65,536) tables (`DP`, Adam `DM/DV`) plus slot pages for tori 2..15; needs `LOG2_ENTRIES>=16`; saves `_dp.npy` alongside the slot files; `scripts/pack_hybrid_gf17.py` packs both parts as GF17 with per-cell scales and writes a dequantized set for `PAGES=` scoring.
- `scripts/ray_hash_field_ti_v8.py` — v3 with `MULTI=a,b` corpora alternating into one table, `SALTED=1` per-corpus hash salt; prints per-corpus hold scores.
- `scripts/pack_trits.py`, `scripts/pack_slots.py`, `scripts/pack_slots_gf17.py`, `scripts/prune_pages_topk.py` — measured packing: quantize, pack (5 trits or 5 GF17 digits per 3 bytes), write, verify round trip, report `os.path.getsize`, emit dequantized weights for eval-only scoring. Best packed file: GF17 per-cell, 14.26 MB, 2.3968 bpb.
- `scripts/build_big_corpus.py` — streams cosmopedia-v2, the-stack-smol-xl / codeparrot-clean, grokipedia into `data/<OUTNAME>/{train,hold}.bin`; `MIX`, `TARGET_GB`, `HOLD_PCT`.
- Data: `data/big` (1.16 GB cosmopedia), `data/big2` (1.79 GB cosmopedia+grokipedia), `data/code` (285 MB code). Best measured: 1.6622 bits/byte on the big2 hold at 2^17 cells × 16 tori.
- Invariants: consume byte t before computing its addresses; one `ti.FieldsBuilder` per big field (Vulkan 4 GiB root buffer); compare arms only at the same `STEPS` schedule.

### 2026-09-20 evening additions (v6.21.12)
- `scripts/ray_hash_field_ti_v11.py`: v9 with the CSR grouping on the GPU (`hist` → `prep` → `scan` ×2 → `scatter` → `compact`; own coalesced three-level scan because Taichi's `PrefixSumExecutor` is wrong on Vulkan on the RX 9060 XT), per-hit atomic slot gradients into `GA`, 8-lane cross-entropy, big fields in their own FieldsBuilders, `DUMP=` per-position loss dump, `SALT0=` hash salt offset, `PROF=1` kernel profiler. 22 ms/step at 2^17, 34 at 2^21, 46 at 2^22 (v9: 108 / 125-159).
- `scripts/ray_hash_field_ti_v12.py`: v11 + level-14 fix (the 4-word torus wrote index 15 in v3..v11, overwritten by the topic torus) + `HOPS` dependent-read tori keyed on a source cell's candidate triple.
- `scripts/ray_hash_field_ti_v13.py`: chained belief hops (`hop(n,h)` reads the running top-3 of `LOG` + last two bytes → address of torus BASE+h; `forward_lvl` adds its cell), word-prefix tori at LEVELS=18 (15 = current word prefix, 16 = prefix + previous word, topic at 17), `SGRID=1` trained-in 4-bit log scale grid, finite guard + gradient clamp in the slot Adam. Recipe: `LEVELS=18 HOPS=4 TERN=1 TERN_SCOPE=cell`; 2^22/30k 1.6797 trained, 399 MB file 1.6753 from disk.
- v13 also: `QLEV=5` five-level slot weights (0, ±half, ±full of the cell max; packer packs 3 quinary digits per cell into 7 bits), raw slot weights saved (earlier engines ternarized at save time), `GEN_PROMPT` beam generator in eval-only mode.
- `scripts/pack_hybrid_tern4.py`: `QLEV=5` quinary slot packing, `DENSE_BITS=8` int8 dense byte1/byte2 tables (lossless; GF17 group-8 was the whole pack loss), `SFIX=lo,hi` fixed scale grid, non-finite slot weights zeroed and counted.
- `scripts/bpb_breakdown.py`: per-byte-class loss from a `DUMP` file; optional two-model probability-average ensemble. N-way stacks are inline numpy over `exports/gf17_continuum/dump_*.npz` (v13e20, v16e20, v13e22, v16 seed1); 2026-09-21 four-way = 1.5645.
- `scripts/ray_hash_field_ti_v14.py`: v13 + rare-word ring tori (RARE table from `data/<corpus>/wordcounts.txt`, LEVELS=21); A/B skipped.
- `scripts/ray_hash_field_ti_v15.py`: v14 + conversation sphere (`SPHERE=N` online-written slot tori addressed by chosen base tori's rays, `sphere_fwd`/`sphere_upd`, `SPHERE_RW` per-source first-sighting weight, `STREAM=1` document-order score-then-learn eval with `STREAM_REPEAT`). Recipe: `SPHERE=8 SPHERE_SRC=3,4,5,7,9,13,15,16 SPHERE_LOG2=22 SPHERE_LR=0.05 SPHERE_RW=0.05,0.05,0.05,0.05,1.0,1.0,0.05,0.05`.
- `scripts/ray_hash_field_ti_v16.py`: v13 + WordNet tori (SYN/HYP tables from `data/<corpus>/synsets.txt`; LEVELS=21 adds synset bigram, hypernym bigram, prefix+synset). Current best recipe: `LEVELS=21 HOPS=4 TERN=1 TERN_SCOPE=cell QLEV=5`; 2^20/150k packs to 135.7 MB at 1.6101 from disk. `throttle.txt` at repo root = seconds slept per training step, read live every 50 steps (0 or missing = full speed).
- `scripts/build_big_corpus.py`: `fineweb` source (HuggingFaceFW/fineweb-edu); `data/big4` = 19.6 GiB. `data/big2/words.txt`, `wordcounts.txt`, `synsets.txt` are the harness tables.
- Invariants: never two GPU jobs; compare arms only at matched STEPS; quote a from-disk bpb only from a packed file reloaded and rescored; kill engines from PowerShell and confirm zero before launching.

## 2026-09-25 Nightly eval (NIGHTLY)
- `scripts/adam_nightly.py`: an orchestrator plus `--phase mem|bpb|chat` child processes. It uses `eval_lock` for split, code and pack provenance.
- Data: `data/concepts/eval/nightly/{nightly_lock.json,f7_smoke_transcript.json,run_<date>.log}`, `report_nightly_<date>[_dry].json` and `nightly_history.json` (prod rows only, key `m` = flat metrics).
- Units: `~/.config/systemd/user/amni-adam-nightly.{service,timer}` (03:30, MemoryMax 8G, CPUQuota 600%, Nice 10). They start `amni-ascend-kpi.service` afterwards.
- KPI: `scripts/v19_kpi.py::nightly()` renders the Nightly block. `render(b,cap)` trims task-board notes to stay under `LIM=3990`.



## 2026-09-30 TORUS-A: torus knowledge space Stage A (Layer 1 exact store + Layer 2 relational torus)
- `crates/adam-torus` (new, standalone crate; build with your own `CARGO_TARGET_DIR`; branch `worktree-agent-a571ac72c9323375f`):
  - `src/lib.rs` is the GF(17) solved retrieval table:
    - Key = (subject, property, idx). An 8-byte tabulation hash gives the shard, the start and a 64-wide 0/1 band. Value = sum mod 17 of the band cells.
    - Build: on-the-fly banded Gaussian elimination over GF(17) (`El::ins`/`back`), sharded at 65,536 keys, with BuRR bumping (buckets of 32, 2-bit thresholds) into a standard-ribbon spill layer (`<file>.l1`).
    - Storage (`GF17RIB1`): 4096x4096 RGBA PTEX pages with one digit per channel, physically one Reffelt pixel word in 17 bits. `Table::get` reads the mmap directly.
    - `Live` = overlay (new values, TOMB deletes) + base.
    - `layered(..., old)` does a shard-local re-solve for compaction.
  - `src/bin/torus_a.rs`: `a1` (build and measure on the Wikidata v3 entity facts), `a5` (learn/forget/compaction), `eps` (slack sweeps).
  - `facts/a5_cc0_facts.tsv`: 500 CC0 invented facts.
  - `py/` is the Layer-2 executable spec (Taichi CPU):
    - `sphere_graph.py` extracts both sphere graphs non-invasively (via a `ti.init` hook);
    - `sphere_noleak.py` rebuilds the sphere without held-out WordNet facts (runs the unmodified script, redirected paths);
    - `torus_data.py` builds the 10-relation dataset;
    - `torus_solve.py` holds positions in Z17^D, per-dim polynomial-LUT relation transforms, sharp distance LUT, integer ICM with cavity weights and mean-field repulsion, eval (hits@10, AUC, recall@20 on both sphere protocols, A3 composition), and the hybrid per-type tori (`--Ds`).
- `crates/adam-gf17`: + `pxw`, `lut17`, `px17`, `pack17`, `unpack17`, `page17` (Reffelt pixel word codec, 17 bits per RGBA GF(17) pixel) + test `px17_roundtrip`.
- Data in `data/torus/`: `a1_wikidata.gf17rib(+.l1)` (216 MB, 45,015,432 facts), `a2_data.npz`, `sphere_graph_{cur,C}.npz`, `sphere_noleak.npz`, `wordnet_graph_noleak.npz`, `torus_final_{A,H}.npz` (GF(17) digits), `res_*.json`, `a5.json`, `a5_overlay.gf17rib`.
- Gates (details in `docs/adam_t/torus_a_results.md`):
  - A1 PASS: 100% exact, 1.046x the bound, 2.06M lookups/s/core, 0 float ops.
  - A2 FAIL: hits@10 1.91x pooled / 2.17x transductive; AUC 0.71-0.85.
  - A5 PASS.
  - A3 PASS: 3.79x.
  - A4: hybrid ties or wins at 6.4% less storage, 27% more solve time.
- Finding: the live `data/concepts/sphere.npz` stores recall@20 0.4057 (current script, 321k-edge graph); the 0.121 in the spec is the older C script's log (1.14M-edge graph). The live sphere was trained on every WordNet edge, which leaks into any WordNet held-out eval that lifts from it.
## 2026-09-30 TORUS-A2b: A2 relational torus re-run (Layer 2 levers + PTEX packing)
- Branch `worktree-agent-a5667e1f0c990af2a` (on `feat/ray-field-serve` 7c7c9b5). Extends `crates/adam-torus`:
  - `py/torus_data.py --a2b`: seed-2930 sampler. Adds auxiliary training-only Wikidata relations (P27/P20/P106) and a degree-constrained, interleaved dev/test split: every held-out entity keeps at least 2 training facts, and every held-out tail is a candidate.
  - `py/sphere_noleak.py --a2b`: no-leak concept sphere for any dataset (all dev and test pairs removed).
  - `py/torus_solve.py` new flags:
    - `--data` and `--mode test` (calibration fitted on dev, test scored once), `--load`;
    - `--mix 1|2|3`: each output coordinate is a GF(17) LUT of 1, 2 or 3 coordinates (17/17^2/17^3 entries), with backoff to the per-dim marginal. The ICM kernel handles mixed maps exactly, and `--mix 1` is bit-identical to TORUS-A.
    - `--bounce tan,nbr --bk --bw`: multi-bounce rays through the sphere's tangent cells or training neighbours;
    - `--lvl`: coarse-to-fine 64/256/1024 cell evidence LUT;
    - `--calib`: per-relation 16-bucket PAV calibration LUT, so P(related) is an integer out of 288;
    - `--pw`: per-relation popularity weight;
    - `--export`: writes every Layer-2 digit array;
    - ECE metrics, and sphere recall from any sphere file.
  - `src/bin/torus_a.rs l2pack`: packs the exported digit arrays (pixel-aligned sections plus a header directory) into 4096x4096 RGBA GF(17) PTEX pages through `adam_gf17::pack17` (`GF17L2P1`, 4.25 bits per digit). It verifies the page view, the per-section decode and 1M random reads bit-exactly.
- Data in `data/torus/a2b/`:
  - `a2b_data.npz`, `sphere_noleak.npz`, `wordnet_graph_noleak.npz`;
  - `torus_dev_base.npz`, and `torus_dev_mix3.npz` (the final model);
  - `luts_test_final.npz`, `l2_test_final/` (exported sections);
  - `layer2_test_final.gf17l2p` (25.3 MB);
  - `res_*.json` and logs.
- Result (test, scored once): hits@10 2.085x, AUC of P(related) 0.907, ECE 0.006, so the A2 gate passes with thin margins, carried by the per-relation popularity weight. Bounce and level levers did not help. Layer 2 costs 72.5 digits per entity. Details: `docs/adam_t/torus_a2b_results.md`.

## 2026-10-05 TORUS-B: unlabeled-ray relation discovery (does the ray define the relation?)
- Branch `worktree-agent-ada5a1c435dedcf1f` (on `feat/ray-field-serve` 521a607). Extends `crates/adam-torus/py`:
  - `torus_solve.py` new flags: `--unlab K` (latent-relation hard EM: each training edge goes to the best of K translation prototypes, relation ids erased), `--warm`, `--sib` (sibling edges, a label-free second-order neighbourhood prior), `--orient` (random orientation control), `--ge`, `--tan`, `--bal`, `--oracle` (dev diagnostic that seeds the true labels), plus `nmi`/`kpp` helpers; argument parsing is now `parse_known_args` so `torus_b.py` can import it.
  - `torus_b.py` (new): ray clusterers (M1 EM prototypes, M2 integer k-modes, M3 coarse hash address), density LUT P(related), popularity-matched negatives, shuffled-position / random-ray / lift-only / metadata controls, NMI and purity, relation inference, direction and inverse analysis, A3-style composition, A2b labeled comparison, PTEX export.
  - `src/bin/torus_a.rs l2pack` reused unchanged.
- Docs: `docs/adam_t/torus_b_prereg.md` (with Amendment 1), `docs/adam_t/torus_b_results.md`, `docs/checklists/checklist_torus_b_v1.md`, `docs/guardian_councils/guardian_council_torus_b.md`.
- Data in `data/torus/b/`: `torus_final_s17.npz`, `torus_final_s23.npz`, `layer2_b_final_s{17,23}.gf17l2p` (20.9 MB, bit-exact), `resb_*.json`, `res_*.json`, `solve_*.log`.
- Result (test, scored once per solver seed 17 and 23): NOT supported. Held-out NMI of unlabeled ray clusters vs true relations 0.128 / 0.131 (gate 0.15; metadata alone 0.448); matched-negative AUC 0.759 / 0.742 (gate 0.85, on par with the labeled A2b ray-only model at 0.744); relation inference above 2x prior on 2 / 1 of 10 relations (gate 6); composition 0.49x / 0.50x (gate 2x). Rays encode relatedness (72% of edges sit in a near-zero-ray cluster) and destination region, not relation type; stored orientation is not used (accuracy 0.51). Storage 64.005 digits per entity, ray-cluster table 3,264 digits.
